1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,202
Welcome to lesson twelve, and to level three.

2
00:00:03,302 --> 00:00:14,128
You'll control where Pods run: on the right hardware, spread across nodes and zones, and away from nodes reserved for other work. You'll also learn to take nodes out safely for maintenance.

3
00:00:15,478 --> 00:00:18,576
For every new Pod, the scheduler does two things.

4
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:29,086
First it filters out nodes that can't run the Pod: not enough resources, a label mismatch, or a taint the Pod doesn't tolerate. Then it scores the remaining nodes and picks the best.

5
00:00:29,186 --> 00:00:36,636
If no node passes, the Pod stays Pending, and the event explains why each node was rejected. Learn to read that line.

6
00:00:36,736 --> 00:00:41,018
Scheduling happens once. Changing things later doesn't move running Pods.

7
00:00:42,368 --> 00:00:49,210
Nodes have labels, just like Pods. Some are set automatically, such as the hostname, CPU architecture and zone.

8
00:00:49,310 --> 00:00:54,440
nodeSelector is the simplest rule: run only on nodes with all of these labels.

9
00:00:54,540 --> 00:01:00,198
Avoid setting nodeName directly. It skips the scheduler, including its resource and taint checks.

10
00:01:01,548 --> 00:01:04,182
Node affinity allows richer rules.

11
00:01:04,282 --> 00:01:12,628
Required rules must match, or the Pod stays Pending. Preferred rules are tried, with a weight, but the Pod is scheduled anyway if they can't be met.

12
00:01:12,728 --> 00:01:18,482
Expressions inside one list are combined with AND. Several selector terms are combined with OR.

13
00:01:18,582 --> 00:01:23,616
And IgnoredDuringExecution means running Pods aren't moved if labels change later.

14
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:29,520
Pod affinity places Pods near other Pods, and anti-affinity keeps them apart.

15
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:35,550
The topology key defines near: the same node, using the hostname label, or the same zone.

16
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:43,516
Watch out for a trap. Required anti-affinity per node, with more replicas than nodes, leaves the extra replicas pending forever.

17
00:01:44,866 --> 00:01:51,500
Topology spread constraints are the modern way to spread Pods. Instead of never together, they say keep it balanced.

18
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:56,970
With six replicas across three zones and a max skew of one, you get two, two and two.

19
00:01:57,070 --> 00:02:04,616
Unlike anti-affinity, this handles more replicas than nodes gracefully. Like all scheduling, it only applies when Pods are placed.

20
00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:09,544
Affinity attracts Pods to nodes. Taints repel them.

21
00:02:09,644 --> 00:02:17,382
A taint on a node says: don't schedule here unless you tolerate this. A toleration on a Pod says: I accept that taint.

22
00:02:17,482 --> 00:02:26,004
NoSchedule keeps new Pods away. PreferNoSchedule avoids the node if possible. NoExecute also evicts Pods that are already running.

23
00:02:27,354 --> 00:02:33,412
Here's a key point. A toleration gives permission to run on a tainted node, but it doesn't send the Pod there.

24
00:02:33,512 --> 00:02:43,090
To dedicate nodes, for example GPU nodes, you need both: a taint on the nodes to keep others away, and a toleration plus a node selector on the Pods.

25
00:02:43,190 --> 00:02:51,504
Also, every Pod tolerates an unreachable node for three hundred seconds. That's why Pods on a dead node are only replaced after about five minutes.

26
00:02:52,854 --> 00:02:56,128
When the cluster is full, which Pods matter most?

27
00:02:56,228 --> 00:03:05,294
A PriorityClass gives Pods a priority value. If a high-priority Pod can't be scheduled, lower-priority Pods may be evicted to make room.

28
00:03:05,394 --> 00:03:10,348
The built-in system classes are for core components like DNS, not for your apps.

29
00:03:11,698 --> 00:03:19,980
For maintenance, cordon marks a node unschedulable, drain evicts its Pods so they're recreated elsewhere, and uncordon brings it back.

30
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:25,690
A PodDisruptionBudget protects an app during drains. Drain waits rather than breaking the budget.

31
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:32,520
But if minAvailable equals the replica count, drains can never finish. And budgets don't protect against crashes.

32
00:03:33,870 --> 00:03:38,744
In the lab, you'll make a Pod that can never be placed and read the scheduler's explanation.

33
00:03:38,844 --> 00:03:44,518
You'll taint nodes, add tolerations, and watch a NoExecute taint evict running Pods.

34
00:03:44,618 --> 00:03:50,756
You'll compare anti-affinity with topology spread, and see a disruption budget block a drain until you fix it.

35
00:03:52,106 --> 00:03:53,396
Let's recap.

36
00:03:53,496 --> 00:03:58,738
The scheduler filters, scores and binds, and Pending events explain every rejected node.

37
00:03:58,838 --> 00:04:03,712
Selectors and affinity attract, taints repel, and tolerations only permit.

38
00:04:03,812 --> 00:04:09,198
Use topology spread to balance Pods, and make sure disruption budgets let drains finish.

39
00:04:09,298 --> 00:04:13,676
Next, you'll make the cluster grow and shrink automatically with autoscaling.
